{"op":"add","path":"/elements/title-block","value":{"type":"TextBlock","props":{"content":"What drives churn in this data?","variant":"heading"},"children":[]}}
{"op":"add","path":"/elements/exec-summary","value":{"type":"TextBlock","props":{"content":"Churn MRR is statistically heterogeneous across plans — both in absolute dollars and per-customer rate — pointing to a genuine plan-tier retention problem rather than a scale artifact (Step 1). The Enterprise plan carries the highest pooled churn rate per customer at $88.13 USD (Step 1). Critically, expansion MRR and churn MRR move together with an overall correlation of 0.984, and this relationship holds within each plan tier, indicating churn is structurally coupled to expansion — high-growth months simultaneously produce high churn, consistent with a cohort-quality or onboarding problem rather than isolated retention failures (Step 2). The churn-to-expansion ratio varies significantly across plans (anova, p=1.85e-23), confirming the coupling intensity differs by segment (Step 2).","variant":"insight"},"children":[]}}
