{"op":"add","path":"/elements/s2-insight","value":{"type":"TextBlock","props":{"content":"Step 1 revealed plan-tier differences in churn severity; Step 2 tests whether churn is an independent retention failure or moves in lockstep with expansion activity. The overall expansion-churn correlation is 0.984 (preferred: pearson), and per-plan regressions confirm the relationship is statistically significant within each tier. The churn-to-expansion ratio varies significantly across plans (anova, p=1.85e-23), with Enterprise median ratio 0.506, Pro 0.661, and Starter 0.954 — meaning the Enterprise plan not only churns most per customer but also converts expansion gains into churn at the highest rate, strongly implicating onboarding or cohort quality rather than pricing (Step 2).","variant":"insight"},"children":[]}}
{"op":"add","path":"/elements/conclusion-block","value":{"type":"TextBlock","props":{"content":"The coupling of churn to expansion across all three plan tiers — and the significantly higher per-customer churn and churn-to-expansion ratio on the highest-risk plan — points to a cohort quality or onboarding problem: customers who expand also churn, suggesting upgrades are not translating into durable value. The recommended next steps are to segment churned customers by cohort vintage and time-to-churn, and to examine whether expansion events (seat additions, tier upgrades) precede churn within the same 30–90 day window, which would confirm an onboarding breakdown rather than a product-fit issue.","variant":"body"},"children":[]}}
{"op":"add","path":"/state/__synthesis","value":{"summary":"Churn MRR is statistically heterogeneous across plans — both in absolute dollars and per-customer rate — pointing to a genuine plan-tier retention problem rather than a scale artifact (Step 1). The Enterprise plan carries the highest pooled churn rate per customer at $88.13 USD (Step 1). Critically, expansion MRR and churn MRR move together with an overall correlation of 0.984, and this relationship holds within each plan tier, indicating churn is structurally coupled to expansion — high-growth months simultaneously produce high churn, consistent with a cohort-quality or onboarding problem rather than isolated retention failures (Step 2). The churn-to-expansion ratio varies significantly across plans (anova, p=1.85e-23), confirming the coupling intensity differs by segment (Step 2).","conclusion":"The coupling of churn to expansion across all three plan tiers — and the significantly higher per-customer churn and churn-to-expansion ratio on the highest-risk plan — points to a cohort quality or onboarding problem: customers who expand also churn, suggesting upgrades are not translating into durable value. The recommended next steps are to segment churned customers by cohort vintage and time-to-churn, and to examine whether expansion events (seat additions, tier upgrades) precede churn within the same 30–90 day window, which would confirm an onboarding breakdown rather than a product-fit issue."}}
